EXT. RIVER CROSSING - FERRY LANDING - DAY

A cable-drawn raft waits on a brown river. A shack. A hitching rail.

AMOS PIKE, 40s, ferryman, coils rope on the dock. He keeps one eye on the road.

CORA LEIGHTON, 30s, a schoolteacher in a travelling coat, arrives with a trunk and sets it down hard. She watches him work.

CORA
How much for the crossing?

AMOS
Two bits.

She pays. He pockets it without looking.

Dust rises on the road. Two riders. SHERIFF WALT DRUMMOND, 50s, and MIGUEL ARROYO, 20s, in handcuffs, slumped over his saddle.

AMOS (CONT'D)
Morning, Sheriff.

DRUMMOND
Amos.

He dismounts. Pulls the prisoner down. Miguel's lip is split. He keeps his eyes on the water.

CORA
Is he hurt?

DRUMMOND
Not badly.

CORA
He looks hurt.

DRUMMOND
Ma'am, it ain't your business.

She turns to Amos.

CORA
Is it?

Amos looks at the river. At the raft. He chews a long moment.

AMOS
Raft only holds four. And a horse.

DRUMMOND
Then we'll make two trips.

AMOS
Then you wait.

Drummond's jaw tightens. Miguel lifts his head and meets Cora's eyes. He mouths something. She nods, barely.

DRUMMOND
Something to say, boy?

Miguel shakes his head. Cora steps between them, calm as a church.

CORA
Sheriff, I'll ride with the prisoner. The ferryman will take the horses first.

DRUMMOND
That wasn't the plan.

CORA
It is now.

Amos grins at the rope. Drummond glares. Then, slowly, he unbuckles his gun belt and hangs it from his saddle horn.

DRUMMOND
Lead on.
